00:03:16.190 --> 00:03:19.669
[END PLAYBACK]

00:03:19.669 --> 00:03:20.780
NIR TARCIC: All right.

00:03:20.780 --> 00:03:23.720
So now that we understand
what we do in order

00:03:23.720 --> 00:03:26.960
to improve everyone's
lives, let's take a second

00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:28.370
and look at our scale.

00:03:28.370 --> 00:03:30.290
What does it take
to power up Waze

00:03:30.290 --> 00:03:32.555
to serve over 80 million users?

00:03:35.450 --> 00:03:37.550
Lets start with the basics.

00:03:37.550 --> 00:03:41.180
Each front-end serving stack
is behind a low bouncer

00:03:41.180 --> 00:03:47.450
and has auto-scaling enabled
to shrink and grow as needed.

00:03:47.450 --> 00:03:50.720
That way, we allow ourselves to
have the flexibility in terms

00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:53.990
of rush hour, or save costs when
it's the middle of the night

00:03:53.990 --> 00:03:57.220
and no one is driving.

00:03:57.220 --> 00:03:59.590
On the database side,
we use Cassandra.

00:03:59.590 --> 00:04:03.910
We have over 800
nodes of Cassandra.

00:04:03.910 --> 00:04:08.260
Each cluster also contains
a Memcached site cluster.

00:04:08.260 --> 00:04:12.960
That Memcached is used to store
temporary data or Cassandra

00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:13.990
data.

00:04:13.990 --> 00:04:17.320
Because Cassandra is not
the most scalable solution

00:04:17.320 --> 00:04:20.680
terms of speed, we
allow our Memcached

00:04:20.680 --> 00:04:24.040
to store the data for us
to grow fast, if needed.

00:04:26.960 --> 00:04:30.560
We currently have over
100 microservices, just

00:04:30.560 --> 00:04:33.080
like this one.

00:04:33.080 --> 00:04:36.410
Each microservice is spread
across multiple availability

00:04:36.410 --> 00:04:38.600
zones and across regions.

00:04:38.600 --> 00:04:41.690
And the most critical
ones are even

00:04:41.690 --> 00:04:43.850
spread across
multiple providers--

00:04:43.850 --> 00:04:47.660
Amazon and Google-- to provide
ourselves the best redundancy

00:04:47.660 --> 00:04:51.490
we can for our users.

00:04:51.490 --> 00:04:53.200
For internal
communications, we have

00:04:53.200 --> 00:04:57.500
developed our own discovery
system and communication.

00:04:57.500 --> 00:04:59.800
This is done through
a peer-to-peer system,

00:04:59.800 --> 00:05:02.950
so servers connect directly.

00:05:02.950 --> 00:05:05.110
They do not have any
single point of failures

00:05:05.110 --> 00:05:06.850
or any bottlenecks for traffic.

00:05:09.400 --> 00:05:13.480
To power up all these 100-plus
microservices and Cassandra

00:05:13.480 --> 00:05:16.120
clusters and everything
else, we currently

00:05:16.120 --> 00:05:20.360
have over 35,000 cores
at any given time,

00:05:20.360 --> 00:05:25.630
growing up to double the
amount in case of rush hours.

00:05:25.630 --> 00:05:29.710
I hope you can see this is
a very large scale and such.

00:05:29.710 --> 00:05:32.220
The thought of
changing providers

00:05:32.220 --> 00:05:33.740
might be a crazy one, even.

00:05:33.740 --> 00:05:36.880
Who does that, right?

00:05:36.880 --> 00:05:39.250
But as one of the folks who
actually did a migration,

00:05:39.250 --> 00:05:41.950
I can say, it's very easy.

00:05:41.950 --> 00:05:43.420
It's easier than you think.

00:05:49.170 --> 00:05:51.570
It's very interesting
because most migration talks

00:05:51.570 --> 00:05:56.755
you'll hear are either bullion,
either this provider, or that.

00:06:00.680 --> 00:06:04.320
A partial migration lets
you mix-and-match provider

00:06:04.320 --> 00:06:05.990
solutions.

00:06:05.990 --> 00:06:09.380
And you can decide to
remain split among providers

00:06:09.380 --> 00:06:11.450
long-term.

00:06:11.450 --> 00:06:15.500
And you can enjoy the benefit
of both worlds at the same time,

00:06:15.500 --> 00:06:17.660
and experiment while you do so.

00:06:20.900 --> 00:06:28.410
For example, in 2015, Amazon had
an outage that lasted 8 hours.

00:06:28.410 --> 00:06:32.270
This was pre our GCP migration.

00:06:32.270 --> 00:06:34.670
We wanted to spin
up our GCP cluster

00:06:34.670 --> 00:06:36.590
sooner than what we wanted.

00:06:36.590 --> 00:06:40.100
But sadly, we couldn't do it,
just because we weren't ready.

00:06:40.100 --> 00:06:44.180
Our engineers work a lot of
time to make sure Waze will not

00:06:44.180 --> 00:06:45.210
crash that day.

00:06:45.210 --> 00:06:46.550
And I'm happy to say we didn't.

00:06:46.550 --> 00:06:49.530
But it was very, very close.

00:06:49.530 --> 00:06:51.290
And this was one of
the triggers for us

00:06:51.290 --> 00:06:56.240
to actually realize we actually
need a multi-cloud solution

00:06:56.240 --> 00:06:58.185
and not just rely
on one provider.

00:07:08.560 --> 00:07:15.690
So Waze was acquired by Google
in 2013, as you probably know.

00:07:15.690 --> 00:07:18.570
Now, if you're owned by a
company who provides cloud

00:07:18.570 --> 00:07:21.900
services, it makes sense
that we'll look at it

00:07:21.900 --> 00:07:27.000
and not just say, we'll stick
with what we have, but doesn't

00:07:27.000 --> 00:07:28.950
mean that we'll actually
migrate to that if it

00:07:28.950 --> 00:07:31.110
doesn't make sense.

00:07:31.110 --> 00:07:34.200
We had that privilege
of picking ourself

00:07:34.200 --> 00:07:36.450
what provider we want to run.

00:07:36.450 --> 00:07:40.697
And we weren't forced
anything upon us.

00:07:40.697 --> 00:07:42.405
It's also very hard
to choose a provider.

00:07:49.470 --> 00:07:51.270
It's also very hard
to choose a provider,

00:07:51.270 --> 00:07:53.280
because there are
so many of those

00:07:53.280 --> 00:07:54.450
and they look differently.

00:07:54.450 --> 00:07:56.300
Right?

00:07:56.300 --> 00:07:58.650
There is this cloud.

00:07:58.650 --> 00:08:00.840
And even this cloud.

00:08:00.840 --> 00:08:04.640
And how about this pretty one?

00:08:04.640 --> 00:08:08.260
And there is even
an actual cloud.

00:08:08.260 --> 00:08:09.420
So how do you choose one?

00:08:14.790 --> 00:08:15.525
So why GCP?

00:08:19.770 --> 00:08:23.836
When you choose-- migrating
to another provider--

00:08:29.170 --> 00:08:30.220
sorry.

00:08:30.220 --> 00:08:32.650
So when you choose to
switch to another provider,

00:08:32.650 --> 00:08:39.940
you have to set yourselves some
key points that you want to do.

00:08:39.940 --> 00:08:41.675
Because you have
to measure that.

00:08:41.675 --> 00:08:43.630
And if you do not
measure that, how

00:08:43.630 --> 00:08:45.220
do you know it's
a successful move,

00:08:45.220 --> 00:08:49.960
or if it even makes
sense for you to do that?

00:08:49.960 --> 00:08:52.360
In our case, when
we evaluate the GCP,

00:08:52.360 --> 00:08:58.210
we choose that cost, resilience,
and end-user performance

00:08:58.210 --> 00:09:01.010
is what matters to us.

00:09:01.010 --> 00:09:02.830
So let's take a look
at each one of those.

00:09:05.900 --> 00:09:08.530
Cost.

00:09:08.530 --> 00:09:11.330
If you want to get
the best price for UVM

00:09:11.330 --> 00:09:15.940
on other providers, you have
to reserve them in advance,

00:09:15.940 --> 00:09:19.390
and figure out how many
you want to reserve.

00:09:19.390 --> 00:09:22.300
Because all our system
uses auto scaling,

00:09:22.300 --> 00:09:24.020
that number was never right.

00:09:24.020 --> 00:09:26.870
We either underestimated
or overestimated.

00:09:26.870 --> 00:09:31.120
It doesn't matter how you
look at it, we lost the money.

00:09:31.120 --> 00:09:34.600
Not to mention that this process
was the very time-consuming.

00:09:34.600 --> 00:09:37.420
Sadly, I did it, so I know.

00:09:37.420 --> 00:09:40.480
We wasted days of work
every quarter just

00:09:40.480 --> 00:09:43.390
to figure out what is that
percentage that we need to buy.

00:09:46.870 --> 00:09:50.200
Not to mention, that
reserving an instance

00:09:50.200 --> 00:09:52.720
means that you're
locked into that one.

00:09:52.720 --> 00:09:55.030
Let's say you have a new
family being released--

00:09:55.030 --> 00:09:56.410
a new CPU family--

00:09:56.410 --> 00:09:57.740
you want to use that.

00:09:57.740 --> 00:09:58.570
Sadly, you can't.

00:09:58.570 --> 00:10:02.440
You're stuck for either
one year or three years.

00:10:02.440 --> 00:10:04.630
What can you do?

00:10:04.630 --> 00:10:06.850
Or maybe you have
redesigned your application

00:10:06.850 --> 00:10:10.574
and now you actually don't need
those 200 nodes of Cassandra

00:10:10.574 --> 00:10:11.740
and you actually need three.

00:10:18.474 --> 00:10:23.580
In GCP we get that
price out of the box,

00:10:23.580 --> 00:10:29.390
because GCP has automatic
sustained use count.

00:10:29.390 --> 00:10:33.110
For us, it made sense that
all our expensive workloads

00:10:33.110 --> 00:10:35.660
should move to GCP
as soon as we can,

00:10:35.660 --> 00:10:38.816
because that will lower
our costs significantly.

00:10:44.060 --> 00:10:47.536
Next up we have
custom machine types.

00:10:47.536 --> 00:10:49.910
For those of you who are not
familiar with custom machine

00:10:49.910 --> 00:10:54.590
types, they allow you to
pick any flavor you want,

00:10:54.590 --> 00:11:00.785
take x cores and x and y
memory, and if you need,

00:11:00.785 --> 00:11:02.330
add a few
[? localize ?] as these.

00:11:02.330 --> 00:11:04.430
But if you don't,
don't add them.

00:11:04.430 --> 00:11:06.200
Each one you can do
whatever you want.

00:11:09.170 --> 00:11:12.240
At first it may not
seem that special.

00:11:12.240 --> 00:11:16.400
But when you're dealing with
a flood of microservices,

00:11:16.400 --> 00:11:18.060
it actually makes sense.

00:11:18.060 --> 00:11:19.880
You have to find
that just balance

00:11:19.880 --> 00:11:22.070
between cost and performance.

00:11:24.620 --> 00:11:26.630
Here on the screen,
we can see an example

00:11:26.630 --> 00:11:30.950
of a Cassandra cluster we have
moved from Amazon to Google.

00:11:30.950 --> 00:11:33.620
That Cassandra
cluster had 6 nodes.

00:11:33.620 --> 00:11:38.300
On the Amazon site, it was in
I2 for Excel, which did the best

00:11:38.300 --> 00:11:43.100
price for it-- is almost 2,500.

00:11:43.100 --> 00:11:46.370
But we actually picked that one
just because we needed the CPU.

00:11:46.370 --> 00:11:49.520
We didn't need all
that local SSD We also

00:11:49.520 --> 00:11:51.500
didn't need all that RAM.

00:11:51.500 --> 00:11:53.210
But you couldn't do
anything with that.

00:11:53.210 --> 00:11:55.970
Because that's what you get.

00:11:55.970 --> 00:11:59.660
We used custom VMs to
exactly pick what we needed.

00:11:59.660 --> 00:12:04.760
We needed 20 virtual CPUs,
and 30 gigabytes of memory,

00:12:04.760 --> 00:12:07.910
and just one local
SSD, which, in Google,

00:12:07.910 --> 00:12:11.700
that's 375 gigabytes.

00:12:11.700 --> 00:12:15.210
As you can see, the
price difference is huge.

00:12:15.210 --> 00:12:18.360
Now, of course we could reserve
those instances in Amazon.

00:12:18.360 --> 00:12:23.100
But still, you can see
there is a huge difference.

00:12:23.100 --> 00:12:28.050
If you combine both of those
materials, reserving instances

00:12:28.050 --> 00:12:33.690
and custom VMs, we have
managed to save Waze over 20%

00:12:33.690 --> 00:12:36.540
every year, just
because of those.

00:12:36.540 --> 00:12:37.260
And that's a lot.

00:12:45.410 --> 00:12:47.870
The next metric we
have was resilience.

00:12:51.750 --> 00:12:55.950
GCP's flat network provide
us a seamless cross-region

00:12:55.950 --> 00:12:57.510
transport.

00:12:57.510 --> 00:13:00.840
A VM can communicate to
a VM in the same region

00:13:00.840 --> 00:13:06.750
just as easily as it can
talk to another region.

00:13:06.750 --> 00:13:10.878
You don't have to worry about
firewall rules, external IPs,

00:13:10.878 --> 00:13:13.940
encryption, or so forth.

00:13:13.940 --> 00:13:17.770
It's as simple as that.

00:13:17.770 --> 00:13:20.410
This is something we
couldn't do in AWS,

00:13:20.410 --> 00:13:23.410
which prevented us
from moving easily

00:13:23.410 --> 00:13:25.055
into a multi-region solution.

00:13:33.530 --> 00:13:34.700
And lastly, we have latency.

00:13:38.080 --> 00:13:40.330
Google's load
balancer is globally

00:13:40.330 --> 00:13:43.840
deployed in all these
locations, giving the best

00:13:43.840 --> 00:13:47.740
latency and performance
for our end users.

00:13:47.740 --> 00:13:51.070
This was a critical path for us,
because Waze currently serves

00:13:51.070 --> 00:13:54.690
over 100 countries worldwide.

00:13:54.690 --> 00:13:57.670
It's not just the US where
you want the best latency,

00:13:57.670 --> 00:14:00.880
but you have to care about
those South America customers

00:14:00.880 --> 00:14:02.740
or those APEC customers.

00:14:02.740 --> 00:14:04.510
Everyone matters, in our case.

00:14:09.070 --> 00:14:11.140
For example, let's
take a service

00:14:11.140 --> 00:14:13.460
that we've migrated into GCP.

00:14:13.460 --> 00:14:16.900
These servers basically
loads up the map

00:14:16.900 --> 00:14:18.310
that you see on the screen.

00:14:18.310 --> 00:14:21.970
Whenever you use Waze,
you get those images.

00:14:21.970 --> 00:14:23.530
It's a very simple application.

00:14:23.530 --> 00:14:26.200
It's a stand-alone one
that on the front end,

00:14:26.200 --> 00:14:28.750
it has NGINX, on the
back end, Tomcat,

00:14:28.750 --> 00:14:32.740
and it's being served by
Cassandra and Memcached

00:14:32.740 --> 00:14:35.590
in order to have all
tiles cached there.

00:14:35.590 --> 00:14:38.860
Memcached currently
has over 99% hit ratio

00:14:38.860 --> 00:14:42.520
because we don't change
the map every second,

00:14:42.520 --> 00:14:44.370
we change it every day.

00:14:47.050 --> 00:14:49.540
Using the same design
we had in Amazon,

00:14:49.540 --> 00:14:53.800
we have managed to improve
our latency by 10%.

00:14:53.800 --> 00:14:58.660
That means 10% lower latency
for all our customers

00:14:58.660 --> 00:14:59.600
just by moving.

00:14:59.600 --> 00:15:02.390
We didn't do any change.

00:15:02.390 --> 00:15:05.590
And, just like this
service, we have dozens more

00:15:05.590 --> 00:15:07.935
that benefit this improvement.

00:15:22.810 --> 00:15:25.960
Sorry about that.

00:15:25.960 --> 00:15:30.070
Once we've decided that
we want to migrate,

00:15:30.070 --> 00:15:32.080
we have set ourselves
some principles

00:15:32.080 --> 00:15:35.350
on how we plan to migrate.

00:15:35.350 --> 00:15:37.750
First of all, we
wanted to make sure

00:15:37.750 --> 00:15:42.160
that we shield our developers
from this migration.

00:15:42.160 --> 00:15:47.350
Our team shouldn't make
the developers' job

00:15:47.350 --> 00:15:52.330
harder because it's more
efficient to run on GCP.

00:15:52.330 --> 00:15:59.110
They should be working and not
stressful about the migration.

00:15:59.110 --> 00:16:00.360
That's not a healthy engineer.

00:16:05.990 --> 00:16:10.330
Next, you should always
have an escape plan.

00:16:10.330 --> 00:16:14.460
In this case, I'm not
sure if he has one.

00:16:14.460 --> 00:16:17.560
In case we have any
issues with the migration,

00:16:17.560 --> 00:16:20.380
we wanted to have
a fast rollback.

00:16:20.380 --> 00:16:25.450
This gave us more confidence in
going fast, because we always

00:16:25.450 --> 00:16:27.190
knew there was a way back.

00:16:27.190 --> 00:16:28.720
We never let it go.

00:16:35.230 --> 00:16:39.400
And, lastly, we're doing
this for our users.

00:16:39.400 --> 00:16:41.580
If we're impacting
our users just

00:16:41.580 --> 00:16:45.285
because we want to migrate,
there's no reason to migrate.

00:16:47.960 --> 00:16:51.860
Let's say you guys are
finishing a long day's work.

00:16:51.860 --> 00:16:56.060
All you want to do is get
home as quickly as possible

00:16:56.060 --> 00:16:59.760
and making sure that Waze
actually works for you.

00:16:59.760 --> 00:17:03.320
Otherwise, you might be
stuck in a traffic jam,

00:17:03.320 --> 00:17:08.660
and you won't get home to spend
time with your family, friends,

00:17:08.660 --> 00:17:10.609
or just relax with
your favorite pet.

00:17:14.150 --> 00:17:16.650
Or maybe you want a cuddly pet--

00:17:16.650 --> 00:17:18.569
up to you-- but you
want to get home.

00:17:26.319 --> 00:17:31.750
Next up, we decided on how
we were going to migrate.

00:17:31.750 --> 00:17:34.810
We needed to design
how we will do this.

00:17:38.050 --> 00:17:40.520
Because we have set
ourselves some principles

00:17:40.520 --> 00:17:43.420
of fast rollback,
we need the solution

00:17:43.420 --> 00:17:48.010
that we can run both
services at the same time.

00:17:48.010 --> 00:17:51.640
We've decided to use
Amazon's and Google's managed

00:17:51.640 --> 00:17:53.410
VPN solutions.

00:17:53.410 --> 00:17:58.300
It made sense-- out-of-the-box
solution-- why not?

00:17:58.300 --> 00:18:01.120
Sadly, that didn't work for us.

00:18:01.120 --> 00:18:04.930
We quickly understood
that Amazon's VPN solution

00:18:04.930 --> 00:18:06.880
has some faults.

00:18:06.880 --> 00:18:09.340
It doesn't scale
as much as we need,

00:18:09.340 --> 00:18:13.360
and when you transmit
large amount of packets,

00:18:13.360 --> 00:18:16.930
it has also a lot
of packet loss,

00:18:16.930 --> 00:18:18.430
both things we
couldn't tolerate.

00:18:23.500 --> 00:18:25.870
We sat down with
Google engineers to try

00:18:25.870 --> 00:18:29.550
and figure out what we can do.

00:18:29.550 --> 00:18:32.880
So we have built a solution
that looks like this.

00:18:32.880 --> 00:18:37.260
On the Amazon side, we've
purchased Direct Connect fibres

00:18:37.260 --> 00:18:42.130
that basically go into
our clinic's data center.

00:18:42.130 --> 00:18:45.280
There, we've
purchased a rack that

00:18:45.280 --> 00:18:49.930
has off-the-shelf VPN
appliances, multiple of those,

00:18:49.930 --> 00:18:53.680
of course, for redundancy.

00:18:53.680 --> 00:18:57.700
From those VPN appliances,
we've established connection

00:18:57.700 --> 00:19:00.580
to Google's managed
VPN solution,

00:19:00.580 --> 00:19:03.385
because that one is scalable
and doesn't have any issues.

00:19:06.110 --> 00:19:10.730
This allowed us to have
both providers look

00:19:10.730 --> 00:19:13.130
like it's just one provider.

00:19:13.130 --> 00:19:18.080
A VM can talk to a VM just
as he talks to a VM meeting

00:19:18.080 --> 00:19:20.510
its own, its region.

00:19:20.510 --> 00:19:23.930
And if it's in GCP,
it doesn't know.

00:19:23.930 --> 00:19:28.070
It has another IP address, but
for him it's an internal IP.

00:19:28.070 --> 00:19:29.190
It just works.

00:19:32.120 --> 00:19:35.700
This later has evolved
into a managed solution

00:19:35.700 --> 00:19:38.040
that Google is
providing customers.

00:19:38.040 --> 00:19:40.980
And it has improved since
we've designed this.

00:19:40.980 --> 00:19:43.200
If you guys want
to use this, you

00:19:43.200 --> 00:19:46.530
don't need those VPN appliances.

00:19:46.530 --> 00:19:48.150
Google's solution
just allows you

00:19:48.150 --> 00:19:52.530
to take a fiber cable from
Amazon and from Google

00:19:52.530 --> 00:19:54.090
and connect them.

00:19:54.090 --> 00:19:54.720
And that's it.

00:19:57.520 --> 00:20:00.660
That product is called
private interconnect.

00:20:07.160 --> 00:20:11.750
So now that we have a reliable
and a low-latency connectivity,

00:20:11.750 --> 00:20:13.275
we can start migrating
our services.

00:20:15.970 --> 00:20:18.130
But we needed to
come up with a way

00:20:18.130 --> 00:20:20.470
to achieve our other principles.

00:20:20.470 --> 00:20:23.440
How do we shield those
engineers from being

00:20:23.440 --> 00:20:25.060
frustrated because we migrate?

00:20:28.030 --> 00:20:30.880
They shouldn't care if it's
in Amazon or in Google.

00:20:30.880 --> 00:20:35.200
They just want to deploy
their applications.

00:20:35.200 --> 00:20:37.420
We've done a lot of research.

00:20:37.420 --> 00:20:39.760
And we found out
that Spinnaker, which

00:20:39.760 --> 00:20:44.770
is an open-source application
developed by Netflix

00:20:44.770 --> 00:20:48.610
and maintained also by
multiple other companies,

00:20:48.610 --> 00:20:51.640
is the right choice for us.

00:20:51.640 --> 00:20:53.950
Spinnaker supports
multi-cloud setup.

00:20:53.950 --> 00:20:57.310
It supports both Amazon
and Google, and even

00:20:57.310 --> 00:21:00.110
the [? server, ?]
if you want one.

00:21:00.110 --> 00:21:03.630
It's maintained
by Google, itself,

00:21:03.630 --> 00:21:07.990
and we even have engineers
from Google working on it here,

00:21:07.990 --> 00:21:09.940
and Microsoft and
other companies.

00:21:13.520 --> 00:21:16.470
As you can see here,
we have an application.

00:21:16.470 --> 00:21:19.600
This is real production traffic.

00:21:19.600 --> 00:21:22.430
In one page, we can
see our application

00:21:22.430 --> 00:21:25.760
running both in
Amazon and in Google.

00:21:25.760 --> 00:21:27.200
We don't need to worry about it.

00:21:27.200 --> 00:21:30.920
We don't need to figure
out where it's running.

00:21:30.920 --> 00:21:35.450
And we can also see that one
instance out of those 120

00:21:35.450 --> 00:21:40.410
shards we have in
Amazon is misbehaving.

00:21:40.410 --> 00:21:44.317
So we can terminate it
easily or alter scaling wheel

00:21:44.317 --> 00:21:44.900
automatically.

00:21:44.900 --> 00:21:46.890
We don't need to worry about it.

00:21:52.440 --> 00:21:57.030
Spinnaker also allows us to
have automatic pipelines.

00:21:57.030 --> 00:22:00.150
In one single pipeline,
we can upgrade

00:22:00.150 --> 00:22:04.140
the binary and configuration
in both environments.

00:22:04.140 --> 00:22:06.520
We don't need to worry about it.

00:22:06.520 --> 00:22:07.525
It's just one click.

00:22:10.230 --> 00:22:14.820
That pipeline produces,
or actually executes,

00:22:14.820 --> 00:22:16.650
more pipelines underneath.

00:22:16.650 --> 00:22:23.820
Each pipeline has canary testing
and fast rollback in case

00:22:23.820 --> 00:22:27.210
there is a problem
in the canary.

00:22:27.210 --> 00:22:31.380
That way, our developers
can deploy fast

00:22:31.380 --> 00:22:35.400
without worrying about if
the code is stable or not.

00:22:39.360 --> 00:22:41.810
If you want to learn more
about continuous deployment

00:22:41.810 --> 00:22:46.010
and how we use Spinnaker in
our production traffic system

00:22:46.010 --> 00:22:49.820
actually, I recommend you
sign up for my session

00:22:49.820 --> 00:22:53.926
that my colleague is giving on
Friday to talk about Spinnaker.

00:22:59.640 --> 00:23:08.340
Now, earlier I talked about
the outage Amazon had in 2015.

00:23:08.340 --> 00:23:11.490
I'm sure most of you will
recognize the headline.

00:23:11.490 --> 00:23:14.622
This was just last week.

00:23:14.622 --> 00:23:16.830
They just gave me more
material for my work, I guess.

00:23:21.000 --> 00:23:25.170
In 2015, we barely
survived that outage.

00:23:25.170 --> 00:23:29.460
Our engineers worked very
hard to maintain ourselves.

00:23:29.460 --> 00:23:33.720
But here, we were just fine.

00:23:33.720 --> 00:23:36.780
Because we've implemented
those principles,

00:23:36.780 --> 00:23:38.580
we've defined ourselves.

00:23:38.580 --> 00:23:42.810
We have multi-cloud, and we
are not bounding ourselves

00:23:42.810 --> 00:23:44.800
to a single provider.

00:23:44.800 --> 00:23:46.860
So in case one goes
down, we're just

00:23:46.860 --> 00:23:49.280
launching instances
in the next one.

00:23:52.550 --> 00:23:58.040
And a lot of companies did
go down, but we didn't.

00:24:06.353 --> 00:24:08.700
I want to summarize a
few things that we've

00:24:08.700 --> 00:24:11.980
learned along the way.

00:24:11.980 --> 00:24:14.140
I think those will
be valuable to you.

00:24:16.810 --> 00:24:19.500
Don't look at it
as a huge migration

00:24:19.500 --> 00:24:25.180
from multimillion company or
a multimillion user company.

00:24:25.180 --> 00:24:28.840
Look at it on a
service-by-service basis.

00:24:28.840 --> 00:24:32.320
You might find that you have any
stand-alone services that you

00:24:32.320 --> 00:24:35.980
can just as easily migrate
them without worrying

00:24:35.980 --> 00:24:40.330
about those big blocks
that have 500 dependencies

00:24:40.330 --> 00:24:42.670
or dozens of dependencies.

00:24:42.670 --> 00:24:48.610
And you can start saving up just
because you've moved that part.

00:24:48.610 --> 00:24:51.370
And if you find that
you evaluate GCP

00:24:51.370 --> 00:24:56.560
and you can't run that
service on GCP, just wait.

00:24:56.560 --> 00:24:58.150
It might get better.

00:24:58.150 --> 00:25:02.860
And I know because we've done
this for the past 3 years,

00:25:02.860 --> 00:25:05.920
and we've been
constantly evaluating GCP

00:25:05.920 --> 00:25:08.510
until the heat-- it has
reached the point where we're

00:25:08.510 --> 00:25:12.010
saying to ourselves,
this is the right moment,

00:25:12.010 --> 00:25:14.704
it's ready for us, let's move.

00:25:21.820 --> 00:25:22.910
So what's next for us?

00:25:25.690 --> 00:25:28.570
We are continuing our
migration, and we're

00:25:28.570 --> 00:25:32.660
hoping to finish it this year.

00:25:32.660 --> 00:25:34.640
We're also looking at
how we can actually

00:25:34.640 --> 00:25:40.880
utilize GCP's infrastructure
for better service,

00:25:40.880 --> 00:25:46.030
to improve our service for our
end users, and our redundancy.

00:25:46.030 --> 00:25:50.660
And as I said earlier,
our users matter.

00:25:50.660 --> 00:25:53.330
Eventually we're
doing it for them,

00:25:53.330 --> 00:25:56.845
not because we want to
run on GCP or on AWS.

00:26:01.500 --> 00:26:03.860
I want to finish
off with this image

00:26:03.860 --> 00:26:08.220
that I found, which
reminds me of how

00:26:08.220 --> 00:26:11.400
I feel with this migration.

00:26:11.400 --> 00:26:14.550
Imagine you are going to the
same arcade shop every day

00:26:14.550 --> 00:26:20.760
for the past years, playing
the same video games every day.

00:26:20.760 --> 00:26:24.850
And then there's a new arcade
shop across the street.

00:26:24.850 --> 00:26:27.430
Who won't want to try that?

00:26:27.430 --> 00:26:31.360
It has newer and
different games.

00:26:31.360 --> 00:26:34.570
So you'll enjoy that.

00:26:34.570 --> 00:26:37.300
But that doesn't mean
you have to only use

00:26:37.300 --> 00:26:40.950
that new shiny arcade shop.

00:26:40.950 --> 00:26:42.660
You can use both.

00:26:42.660 --> 00:26:45.405
What happens if there is a
power failure at the new one?

00:26:45.405 --> 00:26:46.780
You can go back
to the other one.

00:26:50.290 --> 00:26:53.050
You'll just think
for your company

00:26:53.050 --> 00:26:59.290
and for your users what seems
to be the most logical thing

00:26:59.290 --> 00:27:01.160
to do.

00:27:01.160 --> 00:27:03.620
And go with your
heart because that's

00:27:03.620 --> 00:27:08.210
what we did, and we feel more
confident that our service is

00:27:08.210 --> 00:27:11.729
more redundant today and
resilient to any outage

00:27:11.729 --> 00:27:12.395
that we'll have.

00:27:18.360 --> 00:27:22.280
I want to thank you for
your time and for taking--

00:27:22.280 --> 00:27:23.130
for being here.

00:27:23.130 --> 00:27:25.625
And for learning
everything on GCP.

00:27:25.625 --> 00:27:26.840
So thank you.

00:27:26.840 --> 00:27:28.940
[APPLAUSE]

00:27:33.440 --> 00:27:36.790
[MUSIC PLAYING]

